<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">French invasion of Switzerland</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1798">1798</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The French invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> took place in early 1798, when armies of the French Republic overran the Old Swiss Confederacy during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a>. Bern was defeated at Grauholz and fell in March 1798, and resistance in central Switzerland was crushed. The old confederation was replaced by the centralised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>, a French client state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-invasion-of-switzerland.jpg" alt="French invasion of Switzerland" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abraham Girardet · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Entr%C3%A9e_Triomphante_des_Fran%C3%A7ais_dans_Berne,_le_25_Ventose_An_6%C3%A9me_de_la_R%C3%A9publique._G.29220_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> was then a subject territory of Bern, and many of its Francophone people felt oppressed by German-speaking Bern; the patriot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederic-cesar-de-la-harpe">Frédéric-César de La Harpe</a> called a rising in 1795 and had to flee to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In late 1797 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>, fresh from founding the Cisalpine Republic, pressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-directory">French Directory</a> to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, chiefly to secure the Alpine passes into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. Valtellina, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiavenna">Chiavenna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bormio">Bormio</a> seceded to the Cisalpine Republic on 10 October 1797, and the southern Prince-Bishopric of Basel was annexed to France in December.</p><p>The Helvetic Revolution began with a patriot rising at Liestal on 17 January 1798. On 24 January the urban elite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> proclaimed the Lemanic Republic at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>, and more than 40 other short-lived republics followed by April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Invited by the Lemanic Republic, and citing the killing of two French hussars at Thierrens, 12,000 French troops under General Philippe Romain Ménard occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> from 28 January without resistance. A second army under General Balthazar Alexis Henri Schauenburg advanced on Bern. Fighting began on 1 March, Solothurn surrendered, and Bern's government resigned on 4 March. The next day the Bernese lost at Fraubrunnen and Grauholz; their victory at Neuenegg changed nothing, and their commander Karl Ludwig von Erlach was murdered by his own soldiers. Bern lost 700 killed.</p><p>On 12 April 1798, 121 cantonal deputies proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a> under a constitution drafted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-ochs">Peter Ochs</a>. Schwyz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidwalden">Nidwalden</a> and Uri rejected it and raised about 10,000 men under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alois-von-reding">Alois von Reding</a>, but after Schauenburg's counterattack, the fall of Zug and Lucerne and a defeat at Schindellegi, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landsgemeinde">Landsgemeinde</a> of Schwyz gave up on 4 May. The French, impressed by their resistance, let them keep their arms. A revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-valais">Upper Valais</a> was put down in late May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In September 1798 the French under Schauenburg crushed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidwalden">Nidwalden</a> uprising, leaving some 100 dead on both sides and 300 civilians massacred. The conquest of a country neutral since the Revolution strained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a>, helped bring about the Second Coalition, and led to the Austro-Russian campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> of 1799.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “French invasion of Switzerland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
